  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="szekcio__cim">When something does happen</h2>
      <p class="szekcio__lead">
        The real test of a security approach is not what has been built but what happens in the
        first hour. With us, this is not improvisation: every system we operate is under
        continuous, automated monitoring, and an alert follows a defined escalation path.
      </p>

      <figure class="abra">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 880 392" role="img" aria-labelledby="abra-incidens">
          <title id="abra-incidens">
            The six steps of incident handling. Detect: monitoring raises the alert, not the user,
            with threshold- and event-based alerting. Escalate: following a defined procedure, to a
            named person in charge, not to a shared mailbox. Scope: which segment, which machine,
            since when—with a documented environment, this is not detective work. Isolate: the
            affected system is cut off at the dedicated gateway, with no impact on any other client.
            Recover: from backup or from the ready-to-run off-site replica, depending on how
            critical the system is. Document: what happened, what caused it, and what changes so it
            cannot happen again. The specific response time is set in the service agreement.
          </title>

          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="14">WHEN AN ALERT COMES IN</text>

          <path class="abra__vonal" d="M64 44 V324" />

          <rect class="abra__sav" x="88" y="24" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="44" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="48">01</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="43">Detect</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="61">monitoring raises the alert, not the user—with threshold- and event-based alerts</text>

          <rect class="abra__sav" x="88" y="80" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="100" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="104">02</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="99">Escalate</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="117">per a defined procedure, to a named person in charge—not to a shared mailbox</text>

          <rect class="abra__sav" x="88" y="136" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="156" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="160">03</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="155">Scope</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="173">which segment, which machine, since when—in a documented environment, no guesswork</text>

          <rect class="abra__kiemelt" x="88" y="192" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="212" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="216">04</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="211">Isolate</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="229">the affected system is cut off at the dedicated gateway—other clients unaffected</text>

          <rect class="abra__sav" x="88" y="248" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="268" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="272">05</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="267">Recover</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="285">from backup or the ready-to-run off-site replica, based on system criticality</text>

          <rect class="abra__kiemelt" x="88" y="304" width="792" height="44" rx="3" />
          <circle class="abra__pont" cx="64" cy="324" r="7" />
          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="328">06</text>
          <text class="abra__cimke" x="104" y="323">Document</text>
          <text class="abra__kicsi" x="104" y="341">what happened, why, and what changes—so it cannot happen again</text>

          <text class="abra__mono" x="0" y="378">THE SPECIFIC RESPONSE TIME AND SUPPORT WINDOW ARE SET IN THE SERVICE AGREEMENT</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>

      <div class="paros">
        <div>
          <h3>What happens before the alert</h3>
          <p>
            Good operations are not defined by fast fixes. They are defined by most faults never
            turning into an outage. A full disk, a backup that has been failing for months, an
            expired certificate or a server not updated in two years are all problems that automated
            monitoring can catch days or weeks before the consequences hit.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>infrastructure monitoring: network devices, servers, services, capacity and trend tracking</li>
            <li>daily backup verification—not just that the job started, but that it also completed</li>
            <li>regular, automated infrastructure audits and change tracking</li>
            <li>a status and resource report filtered to your own environment</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What remains after recovery</h3>
          <p>
            An incident is over when it is documented. The report is needed not only because an
            auditor or an insurer may ask for it, but because without it, the same fault will happen
            again the same way six months later.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>what happened, when, on which system, and what the root cause was</li>
            <li>what steps were taken, and what their results were</li>
            <li>what changes in the environment or in the procedures</li>
            <li>up-to-date, transferable system documentation—not in the operator’s head</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="kulcs">
        Existing clients can report an issue <a href="/en/support/">on the Support page</a>.
        In urgent cases, by phone.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--mist">
